  "title": "Stimulus range bias explains the outcome of preferred-illuminance adjustments",
  "authors": "Fotios SA and Cheal C, 2010",
  "abstract_summary": "Previous studies suggest that mean preferred illuminances determined using an adjustment task tend to lie in the middle of the range of illuminances available, indicating a stimulus range bias. This hypothesis was validated through an experimental study. The findings suggest that the adjustment task is not an appropriate method for determining optimum illuminances.",
  "methodology": {
    "design": "Within-subjects experimental design with three stimulus ranges and two starting illuminance conditions (min/max). Order of stimulus ranges was counterbalanced.",
    "setting": "Laboratory booth simulating an office/study environment.",
    "stimuli": "Two 300W tungsten halogen lamps providing dimmable illumination. Three stimulus ranges were used: Low (48-1037 lux), Middle (83-1950 lux), High (165-2550 lux).",
    "measures": "Participants adjusted illuminance to their 'preferred' level. Illuminance was measured by experimenter. Correlated Colour Temperature (CCT) was also measured at different illuminance levels."
  },
  "participants": {
    "n_total": 21,
    "demographics": "13 female, 8 male. 19 subjects aged 18-44 years, 2 aged 45-54 years. Naive subjects, paid for participation. Adapted to photopic levels in a daylit space prior to entering the dark test room."
  },
